* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: As you walk through the array, whenever you see a 1, you grow your current streak. If that streak beats your previous record, you update your maximum record. Whenever you hit a 0, the streak is broken, so you reset your counter back to zero and start fresh.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED

Approach 1: Brute Force with Nested Loops

* Where it comes from: Naive baseline where every possible subarray is checked from scratch.
* Idea: Use two loops to examine every possible starting and ending index of subarrays, and count how many consecutive ones exist in each subarray, tracking the maximum.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int max_count = 0;
        int n = nums.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = i; j < n; j++) {
                int current_count = 0;
                bool all_ones = true;
                for (int k = i; k <= j; k++) {
                    if (nums[k] != 1) {
                        all_ones = false;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                if (all_ones) {
                    current_count = j - i + 1;
                    if (current_count > max_count) {
                        max_count = current_count;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return max_count;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n^3) due to the triple nested loops. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better: Re-evaluating subarrays from scratch is extremely redundant. We can track consecutive ones in a single linear pass.

Approach 2: Single-Pass Linear Scan (Your Approach - Optimal)

* Where it comes from: Official LeetCode editorial approach and standard array scanning pattern.
* Idea: Iterate through the array once. Keep a running count of consecutive ones. When you see a 1, increment the count and update the maximum. When you see a 0, reset the count to 0.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(vector<int>& nums) {
        int max_count = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == 1) {
                count++;
                if (count > max_count) {
                    max_count = count;
                }
            } else {
                count = 0;
            }
        }
        return max_count;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) for a single linear pass. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better: While your approach is already optimal in time and space, we can write it more cleanly using std::max and range-based loops.

